# CascadEffects Performance Platform

# Waypoint 28 — Performance Builder Composition Direction & Member Product Model

**Date:** 2026-09-23  
**Status:** ARCHITECTURE / PRODUCT DIRECTION CHECKPOINT  
**Previous Waypoint:** Waypoint 27 — Authentication and Role-Based Entry Foundation

---

# 1. Overview

This Waypoint records the product and architecture direction established after
real-world testing of the Organization Admin Performance Sheet workflow and
the current Builder.

The project has reached the point where the existing Builder should no longer
be treated as the place where every performance feature is independently
implemented.

The new direction is:

> **Organization Administration builds and configures reusable performance
> products. The Performance Builder is the composition layer that calls and
> assembles those products into the organization's performance experience.**

The Builder is therefore a caller / composition surface, not a second product
factory.

This Waypoint exists so the direction is recoverable in the next development
session without relying on conversation memory.

---

# 2. Previous Architecture Preserved

The established core architecture remains intact:

```text
Organization Administration
        ↓
Performance Builder
        ↓
Published Performance Sheet
        ↓
Assignment
        ↓
Monthly Performance Instance
        ↓
Runtime
        ↓
Member / Manager Performance Experience
```

Builder remains responsible for reusable definitions and publishing.

Runtime remains responsible for execution.

The Member Workspace remains a presentation, navigation, and workflow layer
over Runtime.

No second Builder, Runtime, or Member performance engine should be created.

---

# 3. New Performance Builder Direction

The Performance Builder should become the place where an administrator
assembles the performance experience from products already built within
Organization Administration.

The Builder should not independently reimplement every performance product.

Conceptually:

```text
Organization Admin
│
├── Users / Members
│     └── Create OKRs
│
├── Member OKR Sheets
├── Dashboards
├── Tables
├── Charts
└── Reports
        │
        ▼
Performance Builder
        │
        ├── Performance Header
        ├── Member Tabs
        └── Calls / places configured products
```

The Builder therefore acts as a composition / orchestration layer.

---

# 4. Product Family

The performance product family identified for the platform is:

- Member OKR Sheets
- Dashboards
- Tables
- Charts
- Reports

These are reusable platform capabilities.

They should be developed and configured through the appropriate
Organization Admin experiences rather than being duplicated inside the
Performance Builder.

The exact database relationships for these products are not being invented
or implemented by this Waypoint.

They must be designed deliberately when each product is built.

---

# 5. Member Tabs

The term "tabs" in this product direction means **member / performance
context tabs**, consistent with the Version 1 Mint operational experience.

Tabs are not intended to represent organizational sections such as
"Operations", "Leadership", or "Development".

The intended experience is closer to:

```text
Main | John | Sarah | Maria | Team A
```

where the available member/team contexts are generated from actual
organization data rather than hardcoded names.

The platform must never hardcode individual users or teams into the Builder.

---

# 6. Member OKR Creation

Member-specific OKR configuration should begin from the Users / Members
experience.

The intended entry point is:

```text
Organization Admin
        ↓
Users
        ↓
Member
        ↓
Create OKRs
```

The system already knows the organization and member context.

The Create OKRs action should launch the existing OKR / Builder capability
in the context of that member rather than create a second OKR engine.

The member's resulting performance context can then be represented as a
member tab in the assembled Performance experience.

The exact persistence model must continue to respect the existing
Performance Sheet → Published Version → Assignment → Performance Instance
architecture.

Do not introduce a duplicate member-performance data model merely to support
this UI flow.

---

# 7. Performance Header

The Performance Header remains a reusable Performance Sheet configuration.

The current direction is:

```text
Title
Subtitle
Description
Metrics
```

Employee identity, employee role, and role description are not reusable
Performance Sheet header definition fields.

Those belong to the member / Runtime context.

The Builder should not duplicate organization identity merely because the
Builder is opened from an Organization Admin page.

The Organization context is already known from the authorized
Organization Admin workspace.

The Performance Header provides the actual performance-experience context.

---

# 8. Dashboards, Tables, Charts, and Reports

Dashboards, Tables, Charts, and Reports should follow the same product pattern
as Member OKR Sheets.

They are reusable performance products that can eventually be configured
through Organization Administration and then called by the Performance
Builder.

The intended conceptual model is:

```text
Build product
      ↓
Store product definition
      ↓
Performance Builder calls product
      ↓
Product renders in the appropriate member / performance context
      ↓
Runtime provides execution data where applicable
```

The Builder should not become a collection of unrelated implementations for
every visualization or reporting feature.

Future additions should be able to follow the same composition pattern.

---

# 9. Builder Scope for the Next Implementation

The current Builder should be simplified toward:

## Performance Header

Configure the reusable header.

## Member Tabs / Navigation

Provide the navigation shell for the performance contexts that have been
configured through Organization Administration.

## Product Composition

Provide the place where the configured products are called / assembled.

The Builder should not become responsible for independently creating every
Organization Admin product.

This is an architectural direction, not permission to remove working
functionality blindly.

Existing Objective / Key Result / Initiative functionality must be evaluated
against this direction before being removed or relocated.

